  <header class="py-12 md:py-16 lg:py-20 bg-secondary/30">
    <div class="container-article text-center">
      <h1 class="text-balance">Lumbar Support for Tall People: Why Standard Cushions Sit Too Low (2026)</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg md:text-xl text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto animate-slide-up">
        A long torso moves the lumbar curve higher — and a 14&quot; cushion lands underneath it
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="container-article py-12 md:py-16">
    <Byline publishedDate="2026-08-04" updatedDate="2026-08-04" />

    <div class="bg-secondary/40 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-4 mb-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Standard lumbar cushions are 14"–16" tall and place their apex — the thickest point — about 7"–8" above the seat pan, which matches a user around 5'9". A user at 6'4" with a proportional torso carries the lumbar apex roughly 9"–10" above the seat, and a 6'6" user roughly 10"–11". A cushion whose apex lands 2"–3" low presses the sacrum forward instead of supporting L3, which can flatten the lumbar curve rather than restore it. Tall users need either a tall-frame cushion with a repositionable support point, or — better — a chair with a height-adjustable built-in lumbar mechanism.</p>
    </div>

    <Disclosure />

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. Lumbar cushions are the single most-bought ergonomic accessory on Amazon, and for tall users they are also the most frequently misapplied one. The reason is geometric, not a matter of foam quality or price.</p>

      <p>What follows is a spec analysis, not a product test. I have personally used only the Steelcase Gesture, the chair in my apartment. Every cushion assessment on this page is based on published manufacturer dimensions, the mechanical behavior of the support geometry, and reported experience from tall users in communities like r/ergonomics and r/officechair.</p>

      <section>
        <h2>Why a Long Torso Moves the Lumbar Curve Higher</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>TCA's lumbar support analysis reports that standard lumbar cushions stand 14 to 16 inches tall and place their apex roughly 7 to 8 inches above the seat pan, a geometry matched to a user around 5 feet 9 inches. A 6-foot-4 user with proportional torso length carries the lumbar apex approximately 9 to 10 inches above the seat, and a 6-foot-6 user higher still — which is why a standard cushion often loads the wrong spinal segment.</p>

        <p>The lumbar spine has five vertebrae, and the natural inward curve — lordosis — peaks around L3. Every ergonomics guideline that says "support the lumbar curve" is really saying "put the support apex at L3 when seated." That is a location, measured in inches above the seat pan, and it scales with torso length.</p>

        <p>Sitting height — the distance from the seat surface to the top of the head — averages roughly 52% of standing height in adults. At 5'9" (69"), that is about 36". At 6'4" (76"), it is about 39.5". At 6'6" (78"), about 40.5". The lumbar segment scales with that same sitting height, so the apex of the curve rises with it. That is the whole mechanism.</p>

        <p>Here is the part most cushion buyers miss: the shift is not evenly distributed. Height differences between adults come disproportionately from leg length, but the torso still lengthens meaningfully — and if you are one of the tall users whose height is torso-dominant rather than leg-dominant, the offset is larger still. This is the same variable that makes backrest height and armrest height so unpredictable for tall people. A person who is 6'4" with long legs and a 6'4" person with a long torso need lumbar support at genuinely different heights.</p>

        <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border my-6">
