        </div>

        <p class="text-sm"><em>Estimates derived from proportional sitting-height scaling. Individual torso-to-leg ratio shifts these by roughly ±1". Use the measurement procedure below rather than the table alone.</em></p>

        <h3>What happens mechanically when the apex is too low</h3>

        <p>A lumbar support is a load path. It applies a forward-directed force to the spine at one height, and the pelvis and thoracic spine react around it. When the apex sits at L3, the force restores lordosis and the erector spinae muscles can unload — that is the intent.</p>

        <p>When the apex sits 2"–3" lower, it lands near L5 or the sacrum. Now the forward force is applied below the curve's pivot, which rotates the pelvis rather than supporting the spine, and the lumbar segment above it can actually flatten into slight flexion. You get pressure and a feeling of being pushed, without the postural correction. Tall users frequently describe exactly this: the cushion feels like it is jabbing them in the tailbone or shoving their hips forward. That is the geometry telling you it is in the wrong place, not that you need a firmer or softer pillow.</p>

        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm italic">Lumbar support height must match torso length, not user preference. The lumbar curve apex sits approximately 7"–8" above the seat pan at 5'9" standing height, approximately 9"–10" at 6'4", and approximately 10"–11" at 6'6". Standard lumbar cushions measure 14"–16" tall with a fixed apex near the vertical midpoint, placing support 1.5"–3" below the target for users above 6'2".</p>

        <p>Related reading: <a href="/back-pain-spine-height/" class="link-internal">why back pain scales with height →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Read This First: If You Own a Leap, Gesture, or Aeron, Skip the Cushion</h2>

        <p>This is the most important recommendation on the page, and it is a recommendation not to buy anything.</p>

        <p>Premium task chairs already solve this problem in the backrest. Adding a cushion on top of one is almost always the wrong answer, and it usually makes fit worse:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Steelcase Leap and Leap Plus</strong> — height-adjustable lumbar mechanism plus a separate lower-back firmness dial. The adjustable range is designed to move the support point up and down the backrest. If you are tall and the Leap feels wrong, the fix is to slide the lumbar to a higher setting, not to strap a pillow to it.</li>
          <li><strong>Steelcase Gesture</strong> — shaped flexing back with an optional adjustable lumbar accessory that slides vertically on the backrest. This is the chair I actually sit in, and the built-in support does move meaningfully up the back.</li>
          <li><strong>Herman Miller Aeron Size C</strong> — PostureFit SL uses two independently adjustable pads, one for the sacrum and one for the lumbar, with a depth dial for each. It is specifically designed to decouple the two support points.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Why a cushion actively hurts on these chairs: a 2"–4" thick pad pushes you forward off the backrest, which shortens your effective seat depth by the same amount. For tall users, seat depth is already the tightest dimension in the whole chair — losing 3" of thigh support to gain a lumbar bump you could have gotten from the adjustment lever is a bad trade. It also fights the backrest's designed recline geometry, so the chair no longer supports you correctly when you lean back.</p>

        <p>If your premium chair still feels wrong after you have raised the lumbar mechanism to its top setting and confirmed seat height and depth, the problem is likely elsewhere. Start with <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">the correct chair dimensions by height →</a> and work through each measurement before adding accessories.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Honest Problem With Most Lumbar Cushions Sold to Tall People</h2>

        <p>Search "lumbar support for tall people" and you will get the same best-selling memory-foam cushions that appear for every other lumbar query. Everlasting Comfort, QUTOOL, and the dozens of near-identical white-label variants are generic products in the 13"–16" height range with a molded fixed apex. There is no tall variant, no repositionable pad, and in most cases no published apex height at all.</p>

        <p>That is not a criticism of their build quality. It is a statement about differentiation: these products are not engineered for long torsos, and no amount of listing copy that says "ergonomic" changes the geometry. Buying one because it ranks well for a tall keyword is how tall users end up with a drawer of cushions that all felt wrong in the same way.</p>

        <p>Two things genuinely distinguish a cushion for a long torso:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Total frame height above 18"</strong>, so the support structure spans past the average apex position instead of stopping at it.</li>
          <li><strong>A repositionable or adjustable support point</strong>, so the apex can be set at your measured height rather than the manufacturer's assumed one.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Products meeting both criteria are rare. The two below are the only widely available options I have found whose published dimensions actually satisfy them.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Lumbar Cushions That Actually Reach a Tall Lumbar Curve</h2>

