        <h3>McCarty's SacroEase MSE1 Sacro Premium — Highest Confidence for a Tall Apex</h3>

        <p><strong>Dimensions:</strong> 16" x 15" x 19" &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Support:</strong> adjustable steel rungs &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Approx. price:</strong> $150–$200</p>

        <p>The SacroEase is a framed back support rather than a foam pillow, and that distinction is the whole reason it works for long torsos. Based on published dimensions, the frame stands roughly 19" tall — about 3"–5" taller than a typical cushion — and the support is generated by steel rungs that can be adjusted rather than by a fixed molded contour. That means the apex can be set at the height you measured, not at the height the mold assumed.</p>

        <p>The engineering advantage of a rung-based frame over foam is stiffness distribution. Foam deflects under load and the apex migrates downward as it compresses, especially for heavier users where a chair's own <a href="/best-office-chairs-under-500/" class="link-internal">weight rating and frame stiffness</a> already matter. A steel-rung frame holds its geometry under load, so the support point you set is the support point you get at hour six.</p>

        <p>The tradeoff is real: it costs roughly five times what a foam pillow costs, and it is bulkier and more visible on a chair. It also occupies backrest depth, so the seat-depth penalty discussed above still applies. This is the option for a tall user whose chair has a structurally flat back and who has already ruled out replacing the chair.</p>

