        <h3>DMI Extra-Tall Adjustable Lumbar Support Cushion — Budget Option With Real Adjustability</h3>

        <p><strong>Dimensions:</strong> 14" x 21" &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Support:</strong> removable 9" pad, repositionable along the height &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Approx. price:</strong> $30–$40</p>

        <p>The DMI is a 21"-tall back pad with a separate 9" support pad that attaches anywhere along its height. That second detail is what earns it a place here. Instead of a molded apex at a fixed position, you place the support where your lumbar curve actually is — at 8", at 10", or higher if your torso runs long.</p>

        <p>The 21" total height also spans more of the backrest than a standard cushion, which helps on chairs whose own back is short. Based on published dimensions, it covers the lumbar range for users through roughly 6'6" without the pad running out of travel.</p>

        <p>Confidence here is medium rather than high, for two reasons. First, a soft repositionable pad will not hold apex position under load as precisely as a rigid frame — expect some downward migration during a long session. Second, the attachment method has to actually stay put on your specific backrest fabric or mesh, and results vary by chair. At this price, though, it is the cheapest legitimate way to test whether a higher apex is what your back has been asking for before committing to a $180 frame or a new chair.</p>

