        <h3>When a cushion is the wrong purchase entirely</h3>

        <p>A cushion is a patch on a geometry problem. It is the right patch when the chair is otherwise acceptable — correct seat height, adequate seat depth, decent backrest height — and only the lumbar apex is misplaced. It is the wrong patch when the backrest is too short to support your thoracic spine, when seat depth is already 2" short, or when the seat cannot rise high enough for your leg length. In those cases the money is better spent on the chair itself. Start with <a href="/office-chairs-for-tall-people/" class="link-internal">the tall-user chair guide →</a> or the height-specific breakdown at <a href="/office-chairs-for-6-foot-4/" class="link-internal">office chairs for 6'4" →</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How to Measure Your Lumbar Apex Height</h2>

        <p>Four steps. Do this before buying anything, and do it in the chair you actually use.</p>

        <h3>Step 1: Sit in your working posture and let the foam settle</h3>
        <p>Hips fully back against the backrest, feet flat, thighs level or declining slightly. Wait 30 seconds. Seat foam compresses 0.5"–1.5" under load, and every measurement below is taken from the compressed surface, not the showroom height.</p>

        <h3>Step 2: Locate the deepest point of the curve</h3>
        <p>Slide a flat hand behind your lower back and find where the gap between your spine and the backrest is largest. That is your apex. For most people it sits at or just above the belt line, near L3. If you cannot find a gap at all, your lumbar curve is already flattened by the chair — which is itself the diagnosis.</p>

        <h3>Step 3: Measure from the compressed seat surface</h3>
        <p>Run a tape vertically up the backrest and read the height at the apex. Compare to the table above. If your number is 9" or higher, no standard 14"–16" cushion will reach it in its default position.</p>

        <h3>Step 4: Match the number to the product spec, not the marketing</h3>
        <p>A fixed cushion supports at roughly half its height. A 16" cushion supports near 8". If you measured 10", you need an adjustable support point that reaches 10" — full stop. And if your chair has a built-in lumbar mechanism, run it to its highest setting and re-measure before spending anything.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Why do standard lumbar cushions not work for tall people?</h3>
        <p>Standard cushions are 14"–16" tall with a fixed apex near their vertical midpoint, placing support roughly 7"–8" above the seat pan. That matches a user around 5'9". At 6'4", the lumbar apex sits approximately 9"–10" above the seat; at 6'6", approximately 10"–11". The cushion therefore applies force below the curve, near L5 or the sacrum, which rotates the pelvis instead of restoring lordosis.</p>

        <h3>How high should lumbar support sit above the seat for a tall person?</h3>
        <p>At the L3 apex of your own lumbar curve, measured from the compressed seat surface. Proportional estimates: 8"–9" at 6'0", 8.5"–9.5" at 6'2", 9"–10" at 6'4", and 10"–11" at 6'6". Torso-to-leg ratio shifts these by about an inch either way, so measure in your actual chair rather than trusting the table.</p>

        <h3>Should I buy a lumbar cushion if I own a Steelcase Leap, Gesture, or Aeron?</h3>
        <p>No. The Leap and Leap Plus have a height-adjustable lumbar plus a firmness control, the Gesture has a shaped flexing back with an optional sliding lumbar accessory, and the Aeron Size C has PostureFit SL with independent sacral and lumbar pads. A cushion on any of these pushes you forward off the backrest, costs you 2"–4" of effective seat depth, and fights the recline geometry. Adjust the built-in mechanism upward instead.</p>

        <h3>Are there lumbar cushions actually designed for long torsos?</h3>
        <p>Two, by geometry rather than by marketing. The McCarty's SacroEase MSE1 uses a roughly 19"-tall frame with adjustable steel rungs, and the DMI Extra-Tall is a 14" x 21" pad with a removable 9" support that attaches anywhere along its height. The popular memory-foam pillows — Everlasting Comfort, QUTOOL, and their many white-label equivalents — are generic 13"–16" products with no long-torso version.</p>

        <h3>Can a lumbar cushion fix a chair whose backrest is too short?</h3>
        <p>Only partially. A cushion can move the support point higher, but it cannot create thoracic support above the top edge of the backrest. Budget chairs with 20"–22" backrests leave the shoulder blades unsupported for users above 6'2", and no cushion changes that. A 24"–28" backrest solves it structurally.</p>

        <h3>Does a lumbar cushion help with shoulder and upper-back pain?</h3>
        <p>Indirectly at best. Restoring lumbar lordosis reduces compensatory thoracic flexion, which can ease upper-back load. But shoulder pain in tall users usually traces to armrest height and unsupported forearm weight, not lumbar position. See <a href="/shoulder-pain-tall-people/" class="link-internal">shoulder pain in tall desk users →</a> for that mechanism.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
        <p>Lumbar support is a height problem before it is a firmness problem. Measure your apex, compare it to the product's actual support-point range, and be skeptical of any 14"–16" cushion marketed to tall users — the dimension is the tell. If you already own a chair with an adjustable lumbar mechanism, raise it and skip the accessory entirely. If you are on a budget chair with a flat back, the DMI is a cheap way to test a higher apex and the SacroEase is the durable version of the same idea.</p>
        <p>And if the lumbar apex is only one of several dimensions that do not fit, a cushion is treating a symptom. The chair is the problem.</p>
      </section>

